<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- KUAL extension descriptor. The id has to match the directory name under
     /mnt/us/extensions, or KUAL skips the extension without saying why.

     A word on the shape of the actions in menu.json, since JSON allows no
     comments of its own and the reason is not obvious.

     KUAL turns a menu action into a throwaway script

         #!/bin/ash
         { <action> ; } 2>>/var/tmp/KUAL.log &

     and runs it with /bin/sh, with the working directory set to this folder.
     Two things follow, and both have bitten this extension:

     - the action's standard error goes to /var/tmp/KUAL.log and NOWHERE else.
       A script that cannot be started fails there, silently, while the menu
       has already printed the action on the status line — which reads exactly
       like "it launched start.sh and nothing happened". That file is the first
       place to look, always.
     - "the working directory is this folder" is a promise made by a Java
       kindlet on a 2011 device, and a relative action rides on it. The paths
       below are absolute so that nothing depends on it, which is also what
       kindle-dash's own KUAL extension does.

     The leading /bin/sh is the other half: /mnt/us is a FAT partition, the
     execute bit on it is a property of how the partition was mounted rather
     than of the file, and naming the interpreter means the scripts run whether
     or not the bit survived the copy. -->
<extension>
	<information>
		<name>k4-weather</name>
		<version>1.1</version>
		<author>dev-whiterice</author>
		<id>k4weather</id>
	</information>
	<menus>
		<menu type="json" dynamic="true">menu.json</menu>
	</menus>
</extension>
